\title{bdhs: Tools and Data for the Bangladesh Demographic and Health Survey}
\description{
\code{bdhs} covers every round of the Bangladesh Demographic and Health Survey
(1993-94, 1996-97, 1999-2000, 2004, 2007, 2011, 2014, 2017-18 and 2022)
together with the Bangladesh Service Provision Assessments and the 2001
Maternal Health Services and Maternal Mortality Survey.

The package has two halves:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Bundled, ready-to-use data.} Official estimates for every DHS indicator
published for Bangladesh (\link{bdhs_indicators}), the indicator definitions
(\link{bdhs_indicator_catalog}), survey and dataset catalogues (\link{bdhs_surveys},
\link{bdhs_datasets}), cross-round codebooks (\link{bdhs_codebook}), division
crosswalks (\link{bdhs_regions}) and subnational boundaries
(\code{\link[=bdhs_boundaries]{bdhs_boundaries()}}).
\item \strong{Your own microdata, made easy.} The DHS Program licenses respondent-level
data to registered users only and does not allow it to be redistributed,
so it is not shipped with the package. Once you have downloaded it,
\code{\link[=bdhs_import]{bdhs_import()}} registers it in a fast local store, \code{\link[=bdhs_read]{bdhs_read()}} loads any
round and recode in one line, \code{\link[=bdhs_harmonize]{bdhs_harmonize()}} makes variables comparable
across rounds, \code{\link[=bdhs_design]{bdhs_design()}} builds the correct complex survey design and
\code{\link[=bdhs_estimate]{bdhs_estimate()}} computes indicators that are validated against the
official DHS values.
}
